(footprint "Adafruit_FT232H"
	(version 20260206)
	(generator "pcbnew")
	(generator_version "10.0")
	(layer "F.Cu")
	(descr "Adafruit FT232H USB-C breakout (PID 2264), mounted via 2x11 0.1in header strips -- pad grid/board outline adapted from the community footprint at github.com/jgait/adafruit-ft232h-kicad-footprint (cross-checked against Adafruit's own pin descriptions at learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-ft232h-breakout/pinouts and the user-supplied schematic docs/components_FT232H_sch.png), renumbered 1-22 to match this project's existing FT232H_BRK schematic symbol pin numbering exactly: pin1=3V, pin2=5V, pin3=GND, pins4-11=D0-D7, pin12=GND, pins13-22=C9,C8,C7,C6,C5,C4,C3,C2,C1,C0. Only D0-D7 and C0-C3 (as RXF#/TXE#/RD#/WR# in FT245 sync FIFO mode) are wired in this design; the rest (3V, 5V, 2nd GND, C4-C9) are real pad positions intentionally left unconnected, matching Adafruit's own guidance that the 3V/GND pins can be left disconnected. 3D model is the user-supplied lib/Adafruit_FT232H/Adafruit FT232H Original_1.STEP -- the raw STEP has the board modeled lying on its long-edge side with a slight (~7deg) in-plane skew, not flat/right-side-up; the (rotate -97.055deg Z) and (offset 43.48 -38.66 0) were both reverse-engineered empirically (measuring rendered pin-hole pitch/position against the pad grid above) since no CAD alignment reference was provided with the model. Re-verify visually if the STEP file is ever replaced.")
	(tags "FT232H breakout USB-C Adafruit 2264")
	(property "Reference" "REF**"
		(at 0 -22 0)
		(layer "F.SilkS")
		(effects (font (size 1 1) (thickness 0.15)))
	)
